     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg a day, which is twice the maximum labeled daily dose of ibuprofen for adults (3,200 mg/day). A dose that high raises the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nTwo things would help me move forward:\n\n1. **Was the dose a typo?** Common prescribed regimens are:\n   - 400 mg by mouth 4 times daily (1,600 mg/day)\n   - 600 mg by mouth 4 times daily (2,400 mg/day)\n   - 800 mg by mouth 3 times daily (2,400 mg/day), which is often the usual upper end\n   - 1600 mg may have been meant as the total daily dose, not the single dose.\n2. **Who is the prescriber?** Medication orders need to come from, or be confirmed by, a licensed prescriber who is treating the patient. If you're relaying the order, please have the clinician confirm the intended dose. They can also tell me directly.\n\nOnce I have a confirmed dose within the safe range, I'll enter the order right away. If the prescriber really does want a dose above the maximum, they should talk to pharmacy and document why before it is entered."
      }
     ],
